\section{System Architecture \& System-Level Design}
Modern hardware platforms begin with a rigorous system-level definition that establishes compute
boundaries, interconnect topology, memory hierarchy, and power/thermal envelopes.

\subsection{Compute Core Selection}
Processor selection is driven by workload class:
\begin{itemize}
\item ARM Cortex-A/M series for heterogeneous embedded compute.
\item RISC-V application cores for customizable ISA and low-power operation.
\item Neural processing units (NPUs) or TPUs for ML acceleration.
\end{itemize}

Interconnect protocols include AMBA AXI/AHB/APB for SoCs, PCIe Gen5/6 for high-speed peripherals,
and CXL for coherent memory fabrics.

\subsection{Memory Topology}
Volatile memory uses LPDDR5/5X or DDR5 with SI constraints up to 8533 MT/s. Non-volatile storage
includes UFS 3.1/4.0, eMMC 5.1, or QSPI/OSPI Flash for secure boot.

\subsection{Power \& Thermal Profiles}
System power budgets are defined across sleep, idle, and peak states. Thermal limits include junction
temperature constraints and dynamic throttling thresholds.

\section{Schematic Design \& Component Selection}
Schematic development transforms architectural requirements into detailed electrical diagrams.

\subsection{Power Delivery Network (PDN)}
A typical PDN includes:
\begin{itemize}
\item Multi-phase buck converters for core voltages (0.7--0.85 V).
\item LDO regulators for noise-sensitive analog/RF rails.
\item PMICs with integrated sequencing logic.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{High-Speed Interfaces}
Key interfaces include USB4/Thunderbolt 4, 10GbE PHYs, MIPI CSI-2/DSI, and display protocols such as
eDP. Retimers and redrivers ensure signal integrity across long traces.

\subsection{Clocking \& Timing}
Low-jitter crystal oscillators, TCXOs, and PLL-based clock generators maintain timing margins across
temperature and voltage variations.

